@startuml token-refresh
' Two workers refresh the same token pair at the same time.
'
' The question this diagram exists to answer: what does the *loser* do? Not
' "does it fail" — it must not fail — but which token pair does it go on to use,
' and who is allowed to have written it.
'
' Worker concurrency is capped at 2 (PLAN.md §9), so two is the whole problem.
'
' Rendered by `make diagrams` (phase 8). Do not hand-edit the SVG.

skinparam monochrome true
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam ParticipantPadding 12

participant "worker A" as A
participant "worker B" as B
database "token store\n(version attribute)" as DB
participant "token endpoint\n(Google / Strava)" as OAUTH

note across
  **This used to be a correctness problem. It is now a waste problem.**
  The plan was written against Fitbit, whose refresh tokens were single-use and
  rotated: a lost race destroyed a credential and forced a manual re-auth. Neither
  Google nor Strava rotates, so a lost race now costs one wasted refresh. The
  compare-and-swap stays — it is cheap, and it is what keeps the store's contents
  coherent — but do not describe it as load-bearing. See PLAN.md §8.
end note

== both find the access token expired ==

A -> DB : load()
DB --> A : (tokens, **version 7**)
B -> DB : load()
DB --> B : (tokens, **version 7**)

note over A, B
  Same pair, same version. Neither
  knows the other exists.
end note

== both refresh ==

A -> OAUTH : grant_type=refresh_token
OAUTH --> A : access_A, expires_in
B -> OAUTH : grant_type=refresh_token
OAUTH --> B : access_B, expires_in

note over OAUTH
  Both succeed. The refresh token is
  unchanged and still valid — that is
  what "does not rotate" buys.
end note

== the write decides ==

A -> DB : save(access_A, expected_version=**7**)
DB --> A : ok, **version 8**
note right of A
  **Persist before using.** If the process
  dies between the refresh and the write,
  the store must never hold a pair that
  was already spent.
end note

B -> DB : save(access_B, expected_version=**7**)
DB --> B : **conflict** — the row is at version 8

group the losing branch
  B -> DB : load()
  DB --> B : (access_A, version 8)
  B -> B : continue with **A's** tokens
  note right of B
    B's own access_B is simply dropped.
    It is still valid, and using it would
    also work — but then the store and the
    caller disagree about what is current,
    and the next refresh races from two
    different baselines.

    One rule, no exceptions: **whatever is
    in the store is what you use.**
  end note
end

note across
  **Retry the write, never the refresh.** A conflict means someone else already
  did the work. Refreshing again on conflict is how a bounded race becomes an
  unbounded one. `oauth.TokenHolder` expresses this as a return value: `on_refresh`
  is handed the new pair and returns the pair to actually use, so `clients/` never
  learns that a store exists.
end note

@enduml
